# Hindi and Dravida Nadu

*(Interview given by Anna to the Hindi Goodwill Delegation on 11 October 1950.)*

**Chaturvedi:** Our delegation is non-political. We have come only to strengthen peace and friendship. We have heard that you say Hindi should not be allowed to spread and that you call it an Aryan language. Hindi has been formed through the blending of many languages. We believe both that it is in a position to become the common language and that it deserves that position. But we hear that you oppose this, chiefly on the ground that Hindi is an Aryan language. We therefore wish to know your views.

**C. N. A.:** Gladly. Unfortunately, however, you first met those who conduct false propaganda about me.

**Chaturvedi:** No, no. Please do not think so.

**C. N. A.:** People such as Madhava Menon have falsely told you that we oppose Hindi for political reasons and out of hatred for Brahmins. But there are many reasons for saying Hindi must not be imposed. We opposed Hindi even before India became independent. We conducted an agitation against compulsory Hindi.

Those who introduce Hindi say it must be the national language. Rajagopalachari introduced it on that ground. We opposed it then and succeeded. The reason was that our children would have to learn three languages: their mother tongue Tamil, the international language English, and Hindi. That would be difficult.

Moreover, our language is an eminent language admired by all, rich in literature and exceptional beauty. If another language is compulsorily imposed upon us, our distinguished mother tongue will be pushed aside.

People like you have yourselves admitted that Hindi lacks beauty and literary wealth. Why should such a language be placed as a burden upon us?

There are political reasons for our opposition too. You want Hindi to become the language of government, occupying the place now held by English. We cannot accept this.

If people need Hindi and wish to learn it, we have no objection. But insisting that it is the language of government carries a different meaning.

Supporters of Hindi insist upon making it the political language because they regard India as one nation.

**Kanchanlata:** Do you mean India is not a nation?

**C. N. A.:** No, sister. India is not a nation but a subcontinent, a vast territory inhabited by many peoples. A single government cannot prevail here, and likewise one language cannot serve as the language of government. Let me make one thing clear. We do not object to those who consider Hindi necessary for trade or any other form of communication using it. What we cannot accept is the claim that it is the official language and the insistence that every person in this subcontinent must learn it. India is not one nation. On this fundamental matter, we differ in opinion.

**Chaturvedi:** Can you explain that so we may understand clearly? We hear that you demand Dravidasthan. Is it feasible? Dividing India and creating Pakistan has caused great trouble and economic hardship. Do you not see the difficulties of becoming a separate country? If India is divided into Aryavarta, Sikhistan, and Dravidasthan, will we not experience the same losses and hardships caused by partitioning Pakistan?

**C. N. A.:** That is true, but our demand is different. It is not like Pakistan or Sikhistan. Pakistan was a new creation, and Sikhistan is a new demand. Dravidasthan was and remains a country that existed separately.

**Pandey:** What do you mean?

**C. N. A.:** Geographically and historically, the Dravida Nadu we demand has always existed separately and has not been like North India. Great empires, those of Chandragupta, Akbar, and Aurangzeb, existed in the North. Even then, the Dravida Nadu or southern land of which we speak remained separate under its own government.

India came into being only after the British arrived and brought the country under their control. For administrative convenience, the South was joined to it and rule from Delhi was established. Before that, the South, the Deccan, was an independent country.

**Chaturvedi:** What is the purpose of demanding a separate country?

**C. N. A.:** All authority should rest with us. Now we must go to Delhi over everything. Instead, we must have the right to conduct our own trade. No one else should interfere in such affairs; we should manage everything ourselves.

**Pandey:** Is that economically possible?

**C. N. A.:** It is. We possess all the resources necessary to govern ourselves.

**Chaturvedi:** I understand, then, that Dravidasthan is a separate region rich in economic resources. Is that correct?

**C. N. A.:** Yes.

**Chaturvedi:** Even so, I wish to express my personal view. Would it not be better for important services such as railways, post, and transport to remain common? I can travel directly from Prayag to Kanyakumari if everything is under one administration. Otherwise I would have to get down and show a passport whenever I entered each region. Similar difficulties would arise in postal transport. They would not arise under one administration.

**C. N. A.:** True. But because of the inconvenience of showing passports while travelling from Germany to France and France to Italy, do Europeans wish to bring everything under one administration? If we seek a separate country for its many benefits, we must accept minor inconveniences.

**Kanchanlata:** At a time when people speak of one world and a common parliament for the world, can one say, "I shall go separately"? Can unity be disturbed? Are we not brothers living in one family?

**C. N. A.:** I regret to say your analogy is wrong. We are not brothers living in one family but friends residing in one house, friends compelled to remain in one house even though a beautiful separate house is available.

You spoke of world unity. By separating into our own country, we would not sever ourselves from the world. We would take an interest in world affairs. In foreign matters, we would broadly follow India's general position.

**Chaturvedi:** That is, you would remain together in foreign affairs and cooperate with the actions taken by India's government.

**Kanchanlata:** How would that be possible?

**C. N. A.:** It would. Do we not now have the United Nations to attend to world affairs? Similarly, for the whole Indian subcontinent, we could establish a council and determine a common policy on foreign affairs.

**Kanchanlata:** You want every responsibility and authority except foreign affairs to remain with you. Could you not inform the Central Government and obtain increased powers? You might demand greater authority for your province and speak of separation only if the government refused it. How many benefits come from remaining joined to the Indian Government now?

**C. N. A.:** There are no benefits. We are weary of hearing everything you have said.

**Pandey:** You say Andhra, Kerala, Karnataka, and Tamil Nadu should belong to Dravidasthan. How so?

**C. N. A.:** Ethnically, the people living in those regions are Dravidians.

**Apte:** What, then, of Maharashtra? Dravidians live there too; they are called the five Dravidian peoples.

**C. N. A.:** Yes. If they themselves desire it, they should establish a separate country.

**Kanchanlata:** Bengal?

**C. N. A.:** If they desire it.

**Kanchanlata:** If division continues in this manner...

**C. N. A.:** Why, there have been forty-two states, such as Vidarbha!

**Raghavadas:** Very well. It is said that provinces should be divided on linguistic lines.

**C. N. A.:** That is merely to facilitate administration, not in the sense we propose.

**Raghavadas:** In your Dravidasthan, what would be the common language of Andhra, Kerala, and Karnataka?

**C. N. A.:** Telugu in Andhra, Kannada in Karnataka, and Malayalam in Kerala would respectively be their own languages. English could serve as the federal and international language.

**Raghavadas:** How many people know English?

**C. N. A.:** Perhaps ten or twenty per cent.

**Kanchanlata:** That is fewer than those who know the regional languages.

**Raghavadas:** Suppose I am an ordinary citizen and do not know English. Then there would be no place for me in your parliament.

**C. N. A.:** Forgive me. Those who know a regional language would not be barred from the legislature. In each area, its regional language would be the official language.

**Raghavadas:** Then what of English?

**C. N. A.:** I said it would be used for international relations and federal affairs.

**Raghavadas:** As an ordinary citizen, I have no need of the world. You would become interpreters between people like me and the government!

**C. N. A.:** In a democracy, the general public is not a lifeless object. Is not democracy's highest purpose to advance them? Should ignorance therefore continue for many more years? Do you wish ordinary people always to remain merely ordinary?

**Chaturvedi:** What are your views on Brahmins and non-Brahmins?

**C. N. A.:** The Brahmin-non-Brahmin problem here is different from the Hindu-Muslim question in the North. Brahmins consider themselves superior. They possess greater influence in society and therefore wield influence in politics and economics too.

**Chaturvedi:** Is there no way to resolve this problem?

**C. N. A.:** It may be resolved within another five years.

**Kanchanlata:** How can you say that so precisely?

**C. N. A.:** Sister, it expresses our hope. Even now, prominent Brahmin leaders want this problem brought to an end.

**Raghavadas:** What would be the position of Brahmins in Dravida Nadu?

**C. N. A.:** They would live with human rights. Our intention is not to drive them out.

**Pandey:** Have Brahmin leaders here made no effort to resolve this problem?

**C. N. A.:** Had they tried, the problem would have ended very quickly. Even in 1950, for example, we cannot purchase a house in an agraharam inhabited by Brahmins. Nor is that all. There is the temple: worship and similar functions there must be performed through them; we cannot perform them ourselves. The same applies to our ceremonies.

**Kanchanlata:** Even in Europe, priests exist.

**C. N. A.:** There, anyone may become a priest. Likewise, here I could become Abdul Latif if I wished, but I could not become Anantachari. However much Vedic scripture I studied, I could not become a priest here.

**Kanchanlata:** True. The same condition exists in the North.

**C. N. A.:** You have not realised it.

**Raghavadas:** Have individual Brahmins taken an interest in this matter?

**C. N. A.:** They have, but it ended with them. People such as Rajagopalachari, for example, are reformers in their personal conduct. But their reform stopped with them; they failed to make their own community like themselves.

**Chaturvedi:** People filled with humanity would want these cruelties abolished. No one can deny that social justice must be obtained. Is Mahatma Gandhi's philosophy not sufficient for this? Must separation be demanded?

**C. N. A.:** Gandhi gave a good philosophy, but people here twisted it to suit themselves.

He said the country should be a Rama Rajya in the sense of being a good country. But those with the means to propagate the phrase distort it, saying Rama Rajya means a Hindu kingdom governed by varnashrama dharma and four castes.

**Chaturvedi:** That is true.

**C. N. A.:** Gandhi gave them gold. His associates fashioned it into ornaments as they pleased. But people here fashioned it into shackles and fastened them upon our hands.

**Chaturvedi:** Even in Delhi there is resentment toward "Madrasis," a belief that they capture all the jobs. If separation occurred, might they not be expelled by force?

**C. N. A.:** We expect that. When a country is partitioned, such things must be expected.

The General Secretary continued answering many questions. Their substance was as follows: The rights held by foreigners in Dravida Nadu could provide a solution as rapidly as the Brahmin-non-Brahmin problem itself was resolved. Brahmin leaders were now showing concern that this distinction should disappear. If that concern grew, newspapers such as *The Hindu* might explain our demand. We had demanded a separate country even before Pakistan, but our demand had not received adequate explanation among people in the North because English newspapers, being in their hands, had not presented the issue.

As everyone took leave, the delegation invited him to visit North India when he had leisure.

"I have visited before. I shall certainly come when I find the time," the General Secretary replied.

"Our friendship must grow. We are not politicians; we say this personally," the members said as they departed.

This interview was given by Anna to the Hindi Goodwill Delegation on 11 October 1950. The delegation comprised:

1. Chandrapalli Pandey, President, Hindi Sahitya Sammelan, Allahabad.
2. Principal Kanchanlata, Sabar Mala Mahila Vidyalaya, Lucknow.
3. Baba Raghavadas, member of the United Provinces Legislative Assembly.
4. Pandit Sitaram Chaturvedi, Banaras Hindu University.
5. Balchandra Apte, President, Dakshina Bharat Hindi Prachar Sabha, Chennai.
6. Chandrakantar, associated with the Kashi Rani College hostel and private secretary to the delegation.

The delegation's secretary obtained Anna's permission on 10 October 1950, and the interview took place at the *Dravida Nadu* office on 11 October 1950.

*Interview date: 11 October 1950. Source: Dravida Nadu, 15 and 22 October 1950.*
